% Source of truth for the Publications page.
%
% Edit this file, then regenerate the site's data files with:
%   python3 scripts/bib_to_data.py
% That writes docs/_data/publications.yml and docs/_data/commentary.yml,
% which is what the site actually reads. Commit both this file and the
% regenerated .yml files together.
%
% Notes on this format (see scripts/bib_to_data.py for the exact rules):
%  - Plain UTF-8 text in field values (e.g. "é") — no LaTeX escapes needed.
%  - `author` is an "and"-separated list. Each name may be "First Middle Last"
%    or "Last, First Middle"; already-abbreviated initials ("J. D. Treado")
%    are left alone, full first names ("John D. Treado") get abbreviated to
%    initials automatically. The site owner's own name (last name "Treado")
%    is bolded automatically — no markup needed here.
%  - Compound/multi-word surnames ("Di Terlizzi", "van der Berg") MUST use
%    the "Last, First" comma form, e.g. `Di Terlizzi, Ivan` — otherwise the
%    parser can't tell where the surname starts and will misread part of it
%    as a middle initial (this is a fundamental ambiguity in plain-text
%    name parsing generally, not something a smarter heuristic fixes).
%  - Append a literal `*` right after a name (no space) to mark equal
%    contribution, e.g. `J. D. Treado* and D. Wang*`.
%  - `title` is used exactly as written, including trailing punctuation — the
%    page doesn't add a period for you.
%  - `@article` entries become Publications; `@misc` entries become the
%    Commentary list at the bottom of the page (use its `date` field, e.g.
%    `date = {March 2021}`, instead of `year`).
%  - `url` is used as-is if present; otherwise falls back to
%    `https://doi.org/<doi>` if a `doi` field is given.

